.         
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 *
. 
.         * Verbal description of our model: Linear pooled-OLS regression
. 
.         * Hypothesis 4. Having daughters leads to a larger increase in the su
> pport of gender equality for men than for women (with gender equality measure
> d by attitudes). 
.         
.         * Accordingly, we run a linear pooled-OLS regression analysis with ge
> nder_index as dependent variable and the survey wave (syear), the employment 
> status (empl), years of education (educ), years of education squared (educ_sq
> ), migration background (migback) and sample region (sampreg), sex (sex), whe
> ther a child is still living in the same household (same_hh) and number of ki
> ds (no_kids) as independent variables
.         
.         * the identifying assumption is that conditional on giving birth, the
>  gender of the child is exogenous. We believe this assumption is fulfilled, s
> ince it is illegal to tell the parents about the sex of an embryo before abor
> tion can no longer be performed without risking legal punishment in Germany. 
> Furthermore, there is some evidence that female embryos seem to have a higher
>  chance of survival in stressful pregnancies (e.g. Hamoudi & Nobles, 2014) bu
> t this does not seem to meaningfully impact sex ratios at birth in Germany. T
> herefore, we believe the identifying assumption to be fulfilled.
. 
